WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.700
When should you combine pumping

2
00:00:01.700 --> 00:00:04.320
with closure? A difficult-looking language

3
00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:06.080
may expose a familiar nonregular

4
00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:08.880
core after complementing or intersecting

5
00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:10.420
with a simple regular filter.

6
00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:12.620
We will compare unequal block

7
00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:15.260
counts, related inequalities, and perfect

8
00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:17.080
square lengths over a one-symbol

9
00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:18.200
alphabet to learn how to

10
00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:19.100
choose the argument.

11
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:21.440
Let L consist of zeros

12
00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:23.240
followed by ones with unequal

13
00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:25.380
block counts. Directly adding more

14
00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:27.420
zeros often leaves the inequality

15
00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:30.200
true, so an arbitrary pump

16
00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:33.260
direction is unhelpful. Instead suppose

17
00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:35.140
L regular and isolate its

18
00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:37.840
equal-count complement inside the ordered-block

19
00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:38.300
words.

20
00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:41.200
The complement alone contains many

21
00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:43.200
out-of-order words, so it is

22
00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:45.040
not yet the equal-block language.

23
00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:47.560
Intersecting with zero star one

24
00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:49.400
star removes those words and

25
00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:52.320
leaves exactly equal blocks. Regular

26
00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:53.900
closure would make that language

27
00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:56.460
regular, contradicting the pumping proof

28
00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:57.480
we already know.

29
00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:00.540
Now consider zero-block length at

30
00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:02.820
least one-block length. Choose p

31
00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:04.980
zeros followed by p ones,

32
00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:07.200
lying on the boundary of

33
00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:09.820
the inequality. Pumping up zeros

34
00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:11.540
keeps the condition true, but

35
00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:13.180
pumping down makes the first

36
00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:15.060
block shorter than the unchanged

37
00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:16.040
second block.

38
00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:18.980
For matching outer blocks, a

39
00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:21.020
useful witness is p zeros,

40
00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:22.600
one separating one, and p

41
00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:25.140
zeros. Every legal loop lies

42
00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:26.560
in the first zero block,

43
00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:29.120
so removing it changes only

44
00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:31.300
the left outer count. The

45
00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:33.020
unique separator prevents the pumped

46
00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:35.060
word from being reinterpreted using

47
00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:36.420
a different middle-block length.

48
00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:38.760
For unary square lengths, use

49
00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:40.360
a word containing p squared

50
00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:42.640
copies of one. Any legal

51
00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:44.420
repeated piece has length k

52
00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:46.780
between one and p. Pumping

53
00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:48.240
it twice gives length p

54
00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:50.360
squared plus k, so we

55
00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:52.040
need an arithmetic argument that

56
00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:53.360
this new length is not

57
00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:54.120
another square.

58
00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:56.460
The new length is strictly

59
00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:58.340
above p squared and at

60
00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:00.220
most p squared plus p.

61
00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:01.820
That is still below the

62
00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:03.200
next square, p plus one

63
00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:04.560
squared, whose gap is two

64
00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:07.040
p plus one. Therefore the

65
00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:08.880
pumped word lies between consecutive

66
00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:10.500
legal lengths and cannot belong

67
00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:11.260
to the language.

68
00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:14.900
For a numerical illustration, p

69
00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:16.540
equal to three gives length

70
00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:19.260
nine. Pumping a two-symbol loop

71
00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:21.060
once more produces length eleven,

72
00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:23.560
between nine and sixteen. The

73
00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:25.300
general proof works for every

74
00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:26.520
loop length up to p,

75
00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:28.580
not merely for that two-symbol

76
00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:29.000
example.

77
00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:31.980
For complements of palindrome languages,

78
00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:33.840
another closure route is available.

79
00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:36.680
If nonpalindromes were regular then

80
00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:38.360
palindromes would be regular too,

81
00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:40.380
but the witness p zeros,

82
00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:42.620
one, p zeros breaks under

83
00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:44.100
pumping in its first block.

84
00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:46.200
Carefully state which language you

85
00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:47.920
are assuming regular and which

86
00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:49.640
known contradiction you derive.

87
00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:51.720
Things to take note of.

88
00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:53.540
Pick a witness on a

89
00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:55.960
fragile boundary, and choose pumping

90
00:02:55.960 --> 00:02:57.440
up or down to leave

91
00:02:57.440 --> 00:02:59.120
the language rather than preserve

92
00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:01.740
it. When using complement, specify

93
00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:03.480
the alphabet and filter out

94
00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:05.960
unwanted shapes; for numerical languages,

95
00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:07.980
prove a gap containing the

96
00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:08.600
pumped length.
